1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
They are the stuff of fantasy.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:06,000
Or are they?

3
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Who wouldn't want to soar through the sky like Superman?

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Flying through the air is a feeling that's almost too hard to explain.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Or harness the strength of 10 men.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
This thing is incredibly powerful and it's way cool.

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Ready to take a flying leap?

8
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Or swim through the water like Aquaman?

9
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:32,000
It makes you a super swimmer.

10
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:35,000
Today, technology can make anyone a superhero.

11
00:00:35,001 --> 00:00:37,000
So grab your capes.

12
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:41,000
Now, Superhuman on modern marvels.

13
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,240
The human body is a physical wonder.

14
00:01:04,241 --> 00:01:07,240
It's a machine of unfathomable complexity.

15
00:01:07,241 --> 00:01:10,240
But it has its limits.

16
00:01:10,241 --> 00:01:15,240
Superheroes play to our dreams of shedding those limits.

17
00:01:15,241 --> 00:01:18,240
And we relish the fantasy.

18
00:01:18,241 --> 00:01:23,000
But the fantasy is becoming reality,

19
00:01:23,001 --> 00:01:28,000
as today's science and technology are catching up to yesterday's science fiction.

20
00:01:28,001 --> 00:01:31,000
Take super strength.

21
00:01:31,001 --> 00:01:38,000
Now anyone can achieve it just by buckling into a super-powered exoskeleton,

22
00:01:38,001 --> 00:01:43,000
built by the engineers at Raytheon Sarcos in Salt Lake City, Utah.

23
00:01:43,001 --> 00:01:46,000
Their work is funded by the U.S. Army,

24
00:01:46,001 --> 00:01:50,000
which envisions not only making its soldiers stronger,

25
00:01:50,001 --> 00:01:53,000
but also increasing their endurance and lethality.

26
00:01:53,001 --> 00:01:55,000
Oh, it's way cool.

27
00:01:55,001 --> 00:01:57,000
This thing is incredibly powerful.

28
00:01:57,001 --> 00:01:59,340
incredibly powerful and it follows your every move,

29
00:01:59,341 --> 00:02:03,500
so I don't actually have to do much of anything.

30
00:02:03,501 --> 00:02:09,140
Sarkos exoskeleton is composed of over 1,300 separate parts,

31
00:02:09,141 --> 00:02:13,640
made of everything from aluminum to steel to bronze.

32
00:02:13,641 --> 00:02:17,160
The keys to its strength are electrically driven hydraulics,

33
00:02:17,161 --> 00:02:24,500
operating much like muscles, and steel cables subbing for tendons.

34
00:02:24,501 --> 00:02:30,180
It magnifies the user's power, allowing our super soldier Rex Jamison

35
00:02:30,181 --> 00:02:36,380
to continuously lift 250 pounds and never break a sweat.

36
00:02:36,381 --> 00:02:38,620
I feel a tenth of the weight.

37
00:02:38,621 --> 00:02:40,180
It doesn't require a whole lot of effort.

38
00:02:40,181 --> 00:02:42,860
We've done that particular exercise, basically,

39
00:02:42,861 --> 00:02:44,680
till everyone got bored and walked away.

40
00:02:44,681 --> 00:02:54,400
The general objective is to do a lot of work, or go

41
00:02:54,500 --> 00:02:57,500
with little exertion by the person

42
00:02:57,501 --> 00:03:01,500
and little consumption of energy by the machine.

43
00:03:01,501 --> 00:03:13,500
So greater strength with lower effort.

44
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:20,500
Though this complex apparatus weighs 150 pounds,

45
00:03:20,501 --> 00:03:23,500
it's as nimble as it is powerful,

46
00:03:23,501 --> 00:03:27,500
It allows the wearer full and free mobility.

47
00:03:27,501 --> 00:03:30,500
It follows you around without any problem.

48
00:03:30,501 --> 00:03:36,500
I correct in it the same way I would walking upstairs without it.

49
00:03:36,501 --> 00:03:42,500
It's effortless.

50
00:03:42,501 --> 00:03:47,500
This effortless movement is a result of sensors that detect every action,

51
00:03:47,501 --> 00:03:51,500
no matter how subtle, that Rex is making.

52
00:03:51,501 --> 00:03:55,500
Place throughout the arms and legs,

53
00:03:55,501 --> 00:03:59,500
the sensors direct the suit to match every move of the user.

54
00:03:59,501 --> 00:04:03,500
The person has to do nothing more than command it with his movements,

55
00:04:03,501 --> 00:04:05,500
and the machine does all the work.

56
00:04:05,501 --> 00:04:07,500
It has to move like the person does

57
00:04:07,501 --> 00:04:10,500
and not fight his every movement.

58
00:04:10,501 --> 00:04:13,500
To create this synchronized spryness,

59
00:04:13,501 --> 00:04:18,500
Sarkos engineers started with a feat and worked their way up.

60
00:04:18,501 --> 00:04:20,500
You can see down below he's got any

61
00:04:20,501 --> 00:04:25,000
down below he's got ankle actuators that move his his foot up and down.

62
00:04:25,001 --> 00:04:29,540
He's got a rotator for his tibia and fibia, a knee joint.

63
00:04:29,541 --> 00:04:34,380
And we built a lower extremity first that allowed the person to walk, get up and get down,

64
00:04:34,381 --> 00:04:40,980
walk up ladders, walk up ramps, kick balls, and all that sort of thing.

65
00:04:40,981 --> 00:04:46,620
The legs are designed to allow the user to walk normally, but still be able to support the exoskeleton's

66
00:04:46,621 --> 00:04:50,300
own weight, in addition to a payload of over 200 pounds.

67
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:54,500
He can even hold that weight on one leg.

68
00:04:54,501 --> 00:04:58,500
When he moves around, this moves with his leg,

69
00:04:58,501 --> 00:05:00,500
but when he steps down, it actually tightens up,

70
00:05:00,501 --> 00:05:03,500
and it holds the weight of the upper extremity

71
00:05:03,501 --> 00:05:06,500
and the back assembly, so that when he's standing

72
00:05:06,501 --> 00:05:10,500
in a full position powered up, he doesn't have much load on his shoulder.

73
00:05:10,501 --> 00:05:13,500
So the machine's carrying its weight and the loads weight,

74
00:05:13,501 --> 00:05:15,500
and he's carrying his own weight.

75
00:05:15,501 --> 00:05:19,500
The upper half of the exoskeleton works on the same principles as the lower.

76
00:05:19,501 --> 00:05:26,500
is the lower, providing the arm's magnified power

77
00:05:26,501 --> 00:05:30,500
while maintaining a full range of motion.

78
00:05:30,501 --> 00:05:32,500
The shoulder has a large range of motion

79
00:05:32,501 --> 00:05:36,500
much more than the related joint in your leg, which is your hip.

80
00:05:36,501 --> 00:05:39,500
So the shoulder and arm parts of the exoskeleton

81
00:05:39,501 --> 00:05:41,500
don't look like the lower ones.

82
00:05:41,501 --> 00:05:44,500
So when he moves his elbow, the two muscles pull,

83
00:05:44,501 --> 00:05:48,500
the tendons pull, and he gets the range of motion that he'd like.

84
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:57,500
The same thing happens with the wrist and with moving the shoulder in and out and with flexing the shoulder.

85
00:05:57,501 --> 00:06:02,500
Although the exoskeletons intricate hardware is now exposed,

86
00:06:02,501 --> 00:06:09,500
future plans call for encasing it in protective plating, made of anything from steel to Kevlar armor.

87
00:06:09,501 --> 00:06:13,500
It's too early for the Army to say when it will be ready to deploy.

88
00:06:13,501 --> 00:06:18,500
But once fully developed, the exoskeleton promises to revolutionize military operations.

89
00:06:18,501 --> 00:06:20,500
operations.

90
00:06:20,501 --> 00:06:23,500
For starters, it will help free soldiers of the burdensome task

91
00:06:23,501 --> 00:06:28,500
of lugging around rucksacks, weighing as much as 150 pounds.

92
00:06:28,501 --> 00:06:33,500
Those rucks are extremely heavy, and it's really taking a toll on the soldiers.

93
00:06:33,501 --> 00:06:38,500
Whether it's their physical ability to lift something or being able to go long distances

94
00:06:38,501 --> 00:06:43,500
in order to search or destroy the enemy, I think a system like this will definitely help

95
00:06:43,501 --> 00:06:46,500
our war fighters into the future.

96
00:06:46,501 --> 00:06:49,500
But future applications of this super-powered exoskeleton

97
00:06:49,501 --> 00:06:51,500
aren't limited to the military.

98
00:06:51,501 --> 00:06:54,500
You can think of medical products that help cripple people walk,

99
00:06:54,501 --> 00:06:57,500
or you can keeping people safe from overworking themselves,

100
00:06:57,501 --> 00:07:00,500
working in warehouses, working in construction.

101
00:07:00,501 --> 00:07:03,500
As these engineers empower us with super strength

102
00:07:03,501 --> 00:07:09,500
by putting the machine around the man,

103
00:07:09,501 --> 00:07:14,500
others are trying to make us super by putting the machine in the man.

104
00:07:14,501 --> 00:07:18,500
Such is the work of Professor Kevin Warwick

105
00:07:18,501 --> 00:07:22,500
and his colleagues at the University of Redding in England.

106
00:07:22,501 --> 00:07:25,500
Could their pioneering work be the first step

107
00:07:25,501 --> 00:07:29,500
toward transforming us into super cyborgs like Robocop?

108
00:07:29,501 --> 00:07:31,500
And Darth Vader?

109
00:07:31,501 --> 00:07:34,500
We're at a very exciting time being able,

110
00:07:34,501 --> 00:07:37,500
particularly to link humans and technology together.

111
00:07:37,501 --> 00:07:39,500
What we're trying to do is develop new ways

112
00:07:39,501 --> 00:07:44,500
and new interfaces by which humans can receive information

113
00:07:44,501 --> 00:07:47,300
from a machine and give information to a machine.

114
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:53,060
To advance his research over the past decade, Warwick took a radical approach.

115
00:07:53,960 --> 00:07:56,960
During a five-year period, he acted as his own guinea pig,

116
00:07:57,880 --> 00:08:00,820
carrying several surgically implanted computer chips in his body.

117
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:04,840
That's how he earned his mechanical moniker, Mr. Cyborg.

118
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:09,460
The first chip that I had was in 1998.

119
00:08:10,140 --> 00:08:11,740
I've in fact got one of these here.

120
00:08:11,741 --> 00:08:16,500
This was a radio frequency identification device,

121
00:08:16,501 --> 00:08:18,680
and it was implanted in my left arm.

122
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:21,140
At the time, no one had done it before,

123
00:08:21,380 --> 00:08:23,340
so we didn't know if there would be any problems,

124
00:08:23,460 --> 00:08:26,620
any dangers with it, and hence I went for it myself.

125
00:08:27,020 --> 00:08:30,500
What it did was identify me to the computer in my building,

126
00:08:30,501 --> 00:08:33,040
so the computer opened doors for me,

127
00:08:33,080 --> 00:08:35,480
he said, hello, switched on lights, that sort of thing.

128
00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:37,580
But Warwick had much grander goals

129
00:08:37,581 --> 00:08:39,720
than just enabling machines to recognize him.

130
00:08:39,721 --> 00:08:43,260
He wanted the super ability to control them remotely,

131
00:08:44,220 --> 00:08:46,000
using the power of his mind.

132
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,060
So it was back under the knife.

133
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:52,240
This was implanted in my left arm.

134
00:08:52,800 --> 00:08:57,720
The implant itself consists of 100 very, very small electrodes,

135
00:08:57,721 --> 00:09:00,840
and these were fired into my nervous system.

136
00:09:01,480 --> 00:09:04,060
We can still see that the scars from the operation.

137
00:09:04,860 --> 00:09:08,020
There were then wires running from there,

138
00:09:08,120 --> 00:09:09,700
all up the inside of my brain.

139
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:11,720
coming out at this point here.

140
00:09:11,721 --> 00:09:17,720
And what we did was connect on this little gauntlet arrangement.

141
00:09:17,721 --> 00:09:21,720
This is actually a radio transmitter receiver unit

142
00:09:21,721 --> 00:09:25,720
which from this terminal pad here linked to my nervous system

143
00:09:25,721 --> 00:09:30,720
and by radio, my brain signals were brought outside my body.

144
00:09:30,721 --> 00:09:32,720
So when I moved my hand,

145
00:09:32,721 --> 00:09:37,720
my nervous system signals were sent out to this robot hand here.

146
00:09:37,721 --> 00:09:38,720
So as I moved my hand,

147
00:09:38,721 --> 00:09:43,660
As I moved my hand, my brain signals were controlling this robot hand,

148
00:09:44,160 --> 00:09:50,220
but also signals from the computer could be sent down onto my nervous system for my brain to pick up.

149
00:09:50,221 --> 00:09:54,540
So I could feel how much force the robot hand was applying.

150
00:09:56,640 --> 00:10:00,640
One of Warwick's greatest challenges was to simply recognize those signals.

151
00:10:01,740 --> 00:10:04,180
But six weeks of effort got him over the hump.

152
00:10:04,760 --> 00:10:08,700
What we had done essentially was open up a new path into my brain,

153
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:12,720
via the implant, we could then put electric pulses down

154
00:10:12,721 --> 00:10:16,720
onto my nervous system for my brain to understand.

155
00:10:16,721 --> 00:10:19,720
What it felt like was every time a pulse came in,

156
00:10:19,721 --> 00:10:22,720
where there was actually an electric pulse,

157
00:10:22,721 --> 00:10:25,720
it was like a charge running up my index finger.

158
00:10:25,721 --> 00:10:28,720
The more pulses, the more force.

159
00:10:28,721 --> 00:10:31,720
Warwick's implanted ship also allowed him to exercise

160
00:10:31,721 --> 00:10:35,720
mind control over other machines, like this wheelchair.

161
00:10:35,721 --> 00:10:37,720
But the path to superpowers

162
00:10:37,721 --> 00:10:43,100
But the path to superpowers by fusing man and machine cuts both ways.

163
00:10:43,101 --> 00:10:49,060
Instead of computerizing a human, you can also humanize a robot.

164
00:10:49,061 --> 00:10:53,860
This robot represents a baby step in this revolutionary concept.

165
00:10:53,861 --> 00:10:59,280
Via a Bluetooth wireless connection, it's under the control of cells extracted from the brain of a rat.

166
00:10:59,281 --> 00:11:04,640
The output from the rat brain is then used to drive the wheels of the robot.

167
00:11:04,641 --> 00:11:08,640
The only controlling mechanism is the biological brain

168
00:11:08,641 --> 00:11:12,500
with about 100,000 brain cells.

169
00:11:12,501 --> 00:11:15,780
The extracted cells have been deposited on a Petri dish,

170
00:11:15,781 --> 00:11:18,780
where they continue to send electrical impulses.

171
00:11:18,781 --> 00:11:22,820
They start to develop spontaneously connections.

172
00:11:22,821 --> 00:11:28,140
And once they connect together into a network,

173
00:11:28,141 --> 00:11:32,240
then start to communicate with each other, so to speak.

174
00:11:32,241 --> 00:11:34,620
Electrodes embedded in the dish detect this

175
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:39,520
communication and transfer it to the robot.

176
00:11:39,521 --> 00:11:42,840
Even though the crude neurological activity is just a faint shadow of the

177
00:11:42,841 --> 00:11:49,260
rat's fully functional brain, a learning process is in motion.

178
00:11:49,261 --> 00:11:52,220
The ultrasonic sensors on the robot, which give an

179
00:11:52,221 --> 00:11:55,920
indication when the robot comes close to a wall, are sent

180
00:11:55,921 --> 00:11:59,660
back via the electrodes to our rat brain.

181
00:11:59,661 --> 00:12:02,900
And the neuron connections, how they're firing, starts to

182
00:12:02,901 --> 00:12:05,400
strengthen because it keeps doing something.

183
00:12:05,401 --> 00:12:08,200
But like in humans, if you do something habitually,

184
00:12:08,201 --> 00:12:09,700
you get better at doing it.

185
00:12:09,701 --> 00:12:12,140
Well, our little robot, our rat-brained robot,

186
00:12:12,141 --> 00:12:14,540
is exactly the same.

187
00:12:14,541 --> 00:12:17,940
If this rudimentary gadget fusing biologic brain power

188
00:12:17,941 --> 00:12:21,460
and robotics seems a long way from super cyborgs,

189
00:12:21,461 --> 00:12:23,660
you're right.

190
00:12:23,661 --> 00:12:26,520
But his offspring promised to be less primitive.

191
00:12:26,521 --> 00:12:30,560
The physical robot doesn't have to be small and with little wheels.

192
00:12:30,561 --> 00:12:32,800
It could be a vehicle outside.

193
00:12:32,900 --> 00:12:36,440
So we could have the culture driving around a vehicle,

194
00:12:36,441 --> 00:12:40,940
maybe at an airport or in a hotel or in a hospital,

195
00:12:40,941 --> 00:12:45,580
where you're just driving a vehicle from place to place.

196
00:12:45,581 --> 00:12:48,620
Using the same concept, could a man someday

197
00:12:48,621 --> 00:12:51,680
gain superpower by having his brain exercise control

198
00:12:51,681 --> 00:12:54,820
of a powerful robotic body?

199
00:12:54,821 --> 00:12:56,960
Time will tell.

200
00:12:56,961 --> 00:12:59,000
But Professor Warwick's personal preference

201
00:12:59,001 --> 00:13:00,960
is to go the other way.

202
00:13:00,961 --> 00:13:02,880
He'd rather develop a super brain,

203
00:13:02,900 --> 00:13:04,600
by fusing with computers.

204
00:13:04,601 --> 00:13:08,060
I find life as a human pretty dull and boring.

205
00:13:08,061 --> 00:13:10,640
I think there's so many things we could do,

206
00:13:10,641 --> 00:13:13,520
having new senses, having our brains thinking

207
00:13:13,521 --> 00:13:15,180
in many more dimensions.

208
00:13:15,181 --> 00:13:16,700
Certainly that's what I want to do.

209
00:13:16,701 --> 00:13:20,360
Ultimately, I want to have an implant that links my brain

210
00:13:20,361 --> 00:13:24,080
into a computer network so I can have some of those

211
00:13:24,081 --> 00:13:28,820
artificial intelligent abilities that presently computers have.

212
00:13:28,821 --> 00:13:32,220
As high technology promises to grant us super-powered brains

